<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lenape</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Delaware people, Lenni Lenape</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lenape, also called the Delaware, are an Algonquian-speaking Indigenous people whose homeland covered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware River</a> valley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a> and the lower Hudson valley, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a>. They traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Swedish colonists in the 17th century and made treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a>, but lost most of their land, notably through the Walking Purchase of 1737. Pushed westward, most of their descendants now live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lenape.jpg" alt="Lenape" width="320" height="484" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nikater, 1 Feb 2007 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lenape01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Covenant Chain, the alliance made at Albany in 1677 with Governor Edmund Andros of New York and later extended to other colonies, they became the principal Native partner of the English and claimed authority over many dependent peoples, including the <b>Delaware</b> and remnant groups of the Susquehannock.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Company policy required land to be bought from the Indians, but the Dutch took their gifts of sewant and goods as exclusive title while the <b>Lenape</b> bands meant to share, not leave, and the misunderstanding bred violence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Lenape</b> and Wyandot warriors killed American settlers near present-day Stockport, Ohio, in the Big Bottom massacre.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-2">2 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He soon bought the island from the <b>Lenape</b> for goods worth 60 guilders, and New Amsterdam became its capital.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-4">4 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It was essentially a treaty of military alliance between the <b>Lenape</b> Nation and the United States.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-17">17 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1638 they bought land from the <b>Lenape</b> and built Fort Christina, at what is now Wilmington, Delaware, named after Queen Christina.</blockquote><a href="/state/new-sweden">New Sweden</a></li></ul></section>
